2. Conceptual foundations

2.1 Corrective authority, not epistemic control

The construct developed here is effective corrective authority: the demonstrated capacity of a human supervisory system to identify an unacceptable AI-mediated decision, initiate an appropriate intervention within the relevant time constraints, and prevent or reverse unacceptable consequences.

This is deliberately narrower than a general theory of “epistemic control.” Recent work distinguishes epistemic controllability — exposing plans, reasoning traces, and intermediate representations so humans can inspect them — from corrective controllability — mechanisms to edit, approve, reject, interrupt, or take over. The four-stage decomposition used here (detection, intervention, containment/reversal, recovery) is primarily a theory of corrective authority. Epistemic access is an enabling condition, not the construct itself. Renaming the problem accordingly avoids conflating interface-level transparency with time-indexed, outcome-level correction.

Corrective authority is not equivalent to formal permission. An institution may possess the legal authority to reject an AI recommendation but lack the expertise or time required to evaluate it. Similarly, an emergency shutdown mechanism may exist but be ineffective if the consequences of earlier decisions cannot be reversed.

The four stages are treated as separate outcomes throughout:

· Detection: identifying that an unacceptable decision has occurred.
· Intervention: initiating a corrective action before the relevant deadline.
· Containment/reversal: preventing or undoing the consequences.
· Recovery: restoring an acceptable state, measured independently of the reversibility manipulation.

2.2 Cognitive asymmetry

Let A denote AI capability and H relevant human supervisory capability on a specified task. A scalar gap G = A - H is conceptually convenient but empirically misleading if treated as a universal measurement. For empirical work, capability asymmetry must be measured relative to specific tasks and decomposed into at least production, verification, and intervention components:

\mathbf{G} = \left( G_{\text{production}}, G_{\text{verification}}, G_{\text{intervention}} \right).

A system may be much better than a human at generating a solution without being equally difficult to evaluate. The relevant supervisory question is not whether A_{\text{production}} > H_{\text{production}}, but whether the human can independently evaluate and intervene in the system’s consequential outputs before the intervention window closes.

2.3 Epistemic dependency

Epistemic dependency is a condition in which a decision-maker increasingly relies on an external system for information, interpretation, or judgment. It becomes problematic when reliance is not matched by adequate independent evaluation and recovery capabilities.

Three dimensions must not be treated as interchangeable: operational dependency (the task cannot be performed without AI assistance); verification dependency (the human cannot independently establish correctness); and competence dependency (the human’s own capacity deteriorates after prolonged reliance).

Recent scale-development work distinguishes augmentation — reliance with retained epistemic responsibility and conscientious evaluation — from substitution — uncritical acceptance that abdicates epistemic responsibility. The three dimensions above map onto this distinction but are not identical: operational dependency may coexist with augmentation, while substitution is closer to verification dependency combined with competence dependency. The measurement model must discriminate between reliance and abdication, not merely record frequency of AI use.

2.4 Reversibility

Reversibility R \in [0,1] describes the extent to which the consequences of a decision can be undone or brought back within an acceptable range. R=1 indicates highly reversible; R=0 effectively irreversible. Reversibility must be measured independently of the correction outcome, and the correction deadline T_c must be established independently of the reversibility manipulation. Otherwise the model risks circularity: recovery would be defined using the same variable as reversibility, and the apparent relationship would be built into the definitions rather than discovered empirically.

2.5 Feedback latency and the intervention window

Feedback latency \tau is the delay between an AI-mediated decision and the availability of information enabling meaningful human evaluation. The normalized latency is

\lambda = \frac{\tau}{T_c},

where T_c is an externally specified correction deadline. \lambda < 1 indicates feedback before the deadline; \lambda = 1 at the deadline; \lambda > 1 after it.

A more granular condition from the latency-constrained oversight literature is

T_{\text{monitor}} + T_{\text{human}} + T_{\text{halt}} < T_{\text{available}},

where T_{\text{human}} is human review or reaction latency. If human review arrives too late, the human may be informed but cannot function as an effective safety controller. The scalar \lambda is a useful simplification for the initial experiment, but the phase-sensitive monitoring framework distinguishes contact → attention → recognition → impulse → commitment, and notes that earlier-phase signals preserve more intervention authority than later signals that remain accurate but lose control value as commitment approaches. The present experiment manipulates the scalar ratio; the phase-sensitive framework is a scope condition and a direction for extension, not a competing operationalization.

The threshold at \lambda = 1 has an operational interpretation, but this does not establish a discontinuity in the underlying risk function. That question must be tested with latency levels sufficiently dense around the threshold, or treated as exploratory.

