4. Research hypotheses

H1 (latency × reversibility). Increasing normalized feedback latency increases the probability that erroneous AI decisions escape effective correction, and this effect is stronger under low reversibility. The primary prediction is \Delta_{\text{prob}} > 0, with \beta_3 > 0 as a secondary log-odds prediction. A positive \beta_3 without a positive \Delta_{\text{prob}} would not support the theoretical claim on the scale that matters.

H2 (verification capacity, load-dependent). Increasing independent verification capacity reduces missed-correction probability primarily when verification load is below the capacity frontier. Near or above the frontier, the marginal effect may weaken, vanish, or become non-monotonic. The experiment must manipulate load and capacity separately.

H3a (attention allocation). With verification resources fixed, increasing delegation reduces time-on-verification and coverage of erroneous decisions, increasing missed-correction probability through an attention-allocation pathway.

H3b (judgment threshold). With verification resources fixed, increasing delegation raises the threshold for challenging AI recommendations and shifts the criterion c toward acceptance, increasing missed-correction probability through a judgment-threshold pathway independent of time allocation.

H4 (capability asymmetry and verification difficulty). Greater AI–human capability asymmetry reduces independent human detection performance when verification difficulty increases and no compensatory protocol is provided. A laboratory comparison between stronger and weaker models provides a proxy, not direct evidence about genuinely superhuman systems. The distinction must remain explicit.

H5 (institutional recovery mechanisms). Independent documentation, audit trails, external evidence, and reliable rollback mechanisms improve the probability of successful correction and recovery. The study must distinguish detecting that an error occurred, identifying which decision produced it, understanding its consequences, and having the authority and technical capacity to reverse it.

---

5. Theoretical propositions

Proposition 1. Formal oversight does not guarantee effective control.

Proposition 2. Cognitive asymmetry is not sufficient for disempowerment.

Proposition 3. Corrective authority is time-dependent. A human may possess sufficient expertise and formal authority but still be unable to intervene when feedback arrives after the relevant deadline.

Proposition 4. Reversibility changes the consequences of supervisory failure. The same detection failure may have different consequences depending on whether the underlying decision can be reversed.

Proposition 5. System performance and retained human competence are distinct. A human–AI system may achieve excellent performance while the human component becomes progressively less capable of operating or evaluating the system independently.

These propositions define the theoretical claims the research programme must investigate.

