7. Research methodology

7.1 Overall strategy

The research proceeds through three stages.

Phase 0: Formalization and simulation. Specify variables and causal assumptions; define primary and secondary outcomes; simulate candidate statistical models; evaluate power, false-positive rates, confidence-interval coverage, and convergence; select a minimum effect of scientific interest; run a multiverse power simulation under the actual planned random-effects structure.

Phase 1: Controlled pilot. Validate the experimental task; estimate baseline error and detection rates; measure workload and fatigue; test whether reversibility and latency manipulations are understood; estimate variance components and within-participant correlation.

Phase 2: Confirmatory experiment. Preregister hypotheses, primary estimand, and analysis; fix the primary outcome and model; collect the planned sample; conduct blinded or independently automated outcome evaluation where feasible; report all preregistered results, including null and contradictory findings.

7.2 Core experimental design

The initial confirmatory experiment focuses on H1. A within-participant 2 \times 4 design: reversibility R \in \{0.2, 0.9\}; normalized latency \lambda \in \{0.5, 0.9, 1.1, 1.5\}. Eight conditions.

The four latency levels permit investigation around the operational threshold. However, the original design provides only one observation below and one above the threshold (excluding the endpoint), which is insufficient to distinguish a true discontinuity from a steep linear segment. Revision: either add latency levels clustered around 1.0 (e.g., 0.7, 0.9, 1.0, 1.1, 1.3) with sufficient trials per level, or drop the threshold claim from the confirmatory set and treat it as an exploratory model-comparison question. A confirmatory threshold test with four widely spaced levels will have low diagnostic value regardless of sample size.

7.3 Experimental task

A resource-allocation, infrastructure-scheduling, or controlled simulated operational environment. The AI produces decisions, some containing experimentally introduced errors. Participants receive feedback at varying times and must evaluate and correct. Requirements: independently known ground truth; controlled error injection; matched stakes across conditions; defined correction deadline; manipulated reversibility; manipulated latency; recorded detection and intervention. The task does not require understanding the AI’s internal reasoning; it tests whether available evidence and supervisory procedures allow identification and correction of consequential errors.

7.4 Verification capacity and load

Verification capacity is introduced as a subsequent experimental factor or a justified extension, manipulating load and capacity separately. Possible manipulations: limited versus expanded audit time; restricted versus expanded access to independent evidence; availability versus absence of structured audit records; independent external validation versus AI-generated explanations alone. Participant expertise is measured separately. Combining expertise, audit time, and evidence independence into a single composite would make causal interpretation difficult.

7.5 Error prevalence

Error prevalence q must be controlled. If prevalence changes between conditions, participants may change expectations and attention, affecting detection independently of the latency–reversibility relationship. A fixed prevalence in the initial experiment is preferable, with additional prevalence conditions in later studies. Ground truth must be independent of the AI system and its evaluators.

7.6 Statistical analysis and model comparison

The primary analysis uses a mixed-effects logistic regression appropriate for repeated binary outcomes:

\operatorname{logit}\left[P(M_{ij}=1)\right]
= \beta_0 + \beta_1 \lambda_{ij} + \beta_2 (1-R_{ij}) + \beta_3 \lambda_{ij}(1-R_{ij}) + u_j + v_k,

where i indexes trials, j participants, k tasks; u_j and v_k are participant- and task-level random effects.

The primary confirmatory test is \Delta_{\text{prob}} > 0, with \beta_3 > 0 secondary. The analysis reports predicted probabilities, marginal effects at each reversibility level, confidence intervals, model convergence and sensitivity, false-positive rates, detection sensitivity and false-alarm rates, and results under alternative plausible specifications. The probability-scale interaction is calculated explicitly rather than inferred from the sign of the log-odds coefficient.

Model benchmarking. The original simulation used cluster-robust logistic regression while the analysis model is a mixed-effects logistic regression. Version 0.2 reports a direct comparison on identical simulated datasets (150 per scenario; 10 erroneous trials per cell; \beta_3 = 0.5 for power; true \beta_3 = 0 for type-I error).

