The two methods agree closely. The GLMM is unbiased, converges in every replication, and has correct interval coverage. It has a few percentage points more power, so the conclusion that approximately 80 participants are required for \beta_3 = 0.5 holds under these assumptions. Simulation uncertainty is approximately ±4 percentage points with 150 replications per cell.

A correction to the original reporting: the criterion was “two-sided p < 0.05 and \hat{\beta}_3 > 0,” which is a nominal one-sided threshold of 2.5%, not 5%. The observed false-positive rates of 1–3.7% are therefore calibrated, not conservative. A preregistered one-sided test at 5% would gain modest power.

Remaining simulation gaps. No scenario varies miss rate with trial rank; fatigue and learning are not modeled. The linear-versus-threshold comparison is made under a single truth scenario with different effect sizes across contrasts; it is not a model-comparison exercise. The minimum effect of scientific interest remains a scientific choice that simulation cannot make.

7.7 Multiverse power simulation before preregistration

Before preregistration, the power simulation must vary: random-effects structure (intercepts only versus random slopes for \lambda and R); number of erroneous trials per condition (6, 10, 14); baseline miss rate (0.15, 0.30, 0.50); participant-level variance in the interaction effect; plausible attrition and fatigue; and alternative baseline miss rates. The minimum scientifically meaningful interaction is selected before fixing the final sample size. A statistically detectable effect is not automatically a scientifically important effect. The multiverse report should state the minimum detectable interaction across specifications, not a single point estimate.

7.8 Threshold model

Two candidate specifications are compared:

Linear: \operatorname{logit}(p) = \beta_0 + \beta_1 \lambda + \beta_2 (1-R) + \beta_3 \lambda (1-R).

Threshold: \operatorname{logit}(p) = \beta_0 + \beta_1 I(\lambda>1) + \beta_2 (1-R) + \beta_3 I(\lambda>1)(1-R).

The threshold model is confirmatory only if its functional form is preregistered and independently justified. Otherwise it remains exploratory. Given the spacing of latency levels, the threshold comparison is likely exploratory in the initial experiment regardless of preregistration status.

