8. Power analysis and simulation status

The exploratory power simulation reported in Version 0.1 used two reversibility conditions, four latency conditions, ten erroneous trials per condition, 500 repetitions per parameter setting, a nominal 5% level, and cluster-robust logistic regression. The reported power estimates ranged from approximately 0.10 at N=20 and \beta_3 = 0.25 to 1.00 at N=120 and \beta_3 = 1.0.

These figures remain assumption-dependent and are not yet a validated sample-size recommendation. The assumed coefficients are not empirically established effect sizes; baseline error and participant-variance assumptions require pilot validation; the original simulation used cluster-robust logistic regression while the planned analysis is a mixed-effects model; the number of erroneous trials per condition may create substantial workload; fatigue, learning, and trial dependence may alter effective sample size; and type-I error must be re-evaluated under the actual confirmatory model.

The direct GLM-versus-GLMM comparison reduces one source of uncertainty: under the tested conditions, the two approaches agree closely, and the GLMM has slightly better power and correct coverage. The conclusion that N \approx 80 is required for \beta_3 = 0.5 is therefore more defensible than in Version 0.1, but it remains conditional on the assumed effect size, variance components, and trial count. The multiverse simulation described in Section 7.7 is required before the sample size is fixed.

