9. Human competence and long-term dependency

The central experiment measures immediate corrective performance. A system could preserve excellent immediate performance while progressively reducing human competence. This is a second research problem and must be separately powered.

Let H_{\text{assisted}} denote human performance with AI assistance, H_{\text{withdrawal}} performance after assistance is withdrawn, and H_{\text{baseline}} performance before prolonged assistance. A competence-retention measure is

CR = \frac{H_{\text{withdrawal}}}{H_{\text{baseline}}}.

This ratio is meaningful only when the task scale supports a ratio and baseline performance is nonzero. Otherwise a difference score or standardized change should be used. The withdrawal phase introduces a within-subject factor and an order effect; it must be counterbalanced, and the power simulation must be repeated with that structure.

The extension investigates whether prolonged delegation affects independent task performance, error detection without AI, ability to reconstruct decision logic, confidence calibration, and ability to recognize situations requiring external verification. It distinguishes two conditions that must never be conflated: AI-assisted competence (the combined system performs effectively) and retained human competence (the human remains capable of independently performing or supervising after assistance is removed).

Recent work on AI epistemic disengagement distinguishes augmentation (reliance with retained epistemic responsibility) from substitution (uncritical acceptance that abdicates responsibility), and shows that these patterns have different antecedents and outcomes. The competence-retention design should measure which pattern characterizes the participant, not merely whether performance declines.

