A.2 Random seeds and simulation uncertainty

Null scenarios begin at seed = 1000 and increment; comparison scenarios begin at seed = 5000 and increment. Each parameter setting is seeded independently to avoid reuse of random numbers across scenarios. With 150 replications, the Monte Carlo standard error of a power estimate is approximately

\text{SE}_{\text{sim}} \approx \sqrt{\frac{\hat{p}(1-\hat{p})}{150}}.

When \hat{p} = 0.5, \text{SE} \approx 0.041; when \hat{p} = 0.1 or 0.9, \text{SE} \approx 0.024. Consequently, two power estimates differing by fewer than about 4 percentage points should not be treated as substantively different. The figure reported in Version 0.1 — “power approximately 0.81 at N = 80” — should be read as a simulation point estimate, not as a sample-size recommendation with precise sampling precision.

A.3 Known script limitations

First, power_sim.py uses a cluster-robust GLM rather than the mixed-effects model specified in the main text. This is a historical residue from Version 0.1; the comparison in Appendix B addresses the inconsistency directly. Second, the null-scenario decision rule is “two-sided p < 0.05 and \hat{\beta}_3 > 0,” which is equivalent to a one-sided 2.5% level rather than the one-sided 5% level anticipated in the main text. This makes the reported null rejection rates conservative, not inflated. Third, the scripts do not model fatigue, learning, or trial-order effects; these are explicitly listed as pending simulation extensions in Appendix F.

