B.3 Interpretation and boundary conditions

The two methods are highly consistent in point estimation and inference. The GLMM failed to converge in none of the four scenarios, produced mean estimates close to the true values, and yielded 95% interval coverage between 93.3% and 98.0%. The GLMM has slightly higher power (5 percentage points higher at N=40, \beta_3=0.5; 4 percentage points higher at N=80), which is in the expected direction: the GLMM exploits the variance structure of the random effects, whereas the cluster-robust GLM only corrects standard errors at the cluster level.

Boundary conditions must be stated explicitly. First, these results are based on 10 erroneous trials per cell and 40 task levels, with 40 or 80 participant-level clusters. The methodological literature indicates that when the number of clusters falls below roughly 30–50, GLMM Wald tests can exhibit inflated Type I error and require small-sample corrections or degrees-of-freedom-adjusted tests. The minimum planned sample size here, N=40, lies near that boundary. Appendix B results therefore cannot be extrapolated to N < 30, and the sample-size recommendation in the main text should be read as conditional on “N \ge 40 with a non-sparse clustering structure.” Second, the simulation includes only random intercepts. If the final model adds participant-level random slopes (for example, allowing the \lambda effect to vary across people), the GLMM’s convergence and power characteristics may change; the multiverse simulation required in Appendix F must cover that structure.

