Appendix C: Operationalization of verification capacity and load

C.1 Why a single composite is inappropriate

Section 3.3 reframes verification capacity as a load-dependent, capacity-constrained quantity and introduces the Epistemic Load Ratio (ELR) and Verification Capacity Frontier from the verification-bottleneck framework. The direct methodological consequence of this reframing is that verification capacity is not a latent variable that can be formed by simply summing several indicators. Verification delay grows convexly with load, review quality may deteriorate under overload, and the joint effect of both on “missed correction” depends on which side of the capacity frontier the system occupies. Experimental manipulation must therefore treat load and capacity as two independent factors, not collapse them into a single “verification resources” variable.

C.2 Operational components and manipulation proposals

Component Operational definition Experimental manipulation Measurement
Verification capacity Maximum number of decisions that can receive independent review per unit time Audit time budget (high/low); number of available independent evidence channels Review coverage rate; mean review time
Verification load Number of AI decisions arriving per unit time that require review Decision generation rate; proportion requiring review ELR ≈ load / capacity
Review quality Conditional probability that a reviewer detects an error given available time Overload vs. non-overload conditions Detection rate, d', false-alarm rate
Verification delay Time from decision generation to actionable feedback Manipulated interactively with load level Delay distribution; \lambda

Key design constraint. Load manipulation and latency manipulation must be conceptually orthogonal. If high-load conditions automatically lengthen delays, the effects of load and latency cannot be separated. The recommended approach is to vary review capacity while holding decision arrival rate constant (for example, by reducing the number of available independent evidence channels), or to vary arrival rate while holding review capacity constant. Both manipulations affect ELR, but through different causal pathways, and thus permit testing the capacity-frontier hypothesis: when ELR exceeds some critical value, the marginal effect of added capacity on the missed-correction probability should approach zero.

C.3 Relation to H2

The revised H2 predicts a conditional, possibly non-monotonic relationship. Operationally, this means the analysis should not estimate only a capacity main effect, but should estimate a capacity × load interaction, or use a segmented model to test for a slope difference on either side of an ELR threshold. If the data support a smooth monotonic relationship, the capacity-frontier hypothesis is not supported under that operationalization; if the data support a marked decline in slope as ELR approaches 1, the load-dependent reformulation is supported. Either result must be reported, because the revision of H2 was undertaken precisely to make a potentially failing theoretical prediction testable.

