Appendix E: Procedure for determining the minimum scientifically meaningful effect

E.1 Why simulation cannot replace this decision

Section 7.7 requires that “the minimum scientifically meaningful interaction be selected before the final sample size is fixed.” This is a scientific judgment, not a statistical estimate. Simulation can tell a researcher “how likely it is to detect an effect of a given size with a given sample,” but it cannot tell a researcher “how large an effect is worth detecting.” The common consequence of confusing the two is choosing an effect size because “the simulation shows 80% power” and then mistaking it for a scientifically important threshold.

E.2 Recommended determination procedure

Version 0.2 adopts a three-source anchoring procedure:

First, theoretical anchoring. The theoretical claim in H1 is that the increment in correction failure produced by delayed feedback under irreversible conditions is substantively meaningful on the probability scale. A defensible starting point is to set the delay effect under irreversible conditions at 1.5 to 2 times the delay effect under reversible conditions. This is not an empirical estimate but a theoretical statement: if irreversibility amplifies the delay effect by only 10%, the practical significance of the claim that “irreversibility changes the structure of correction failure” is limited; if it amplifies by a factor of 2 or more, the mechanism is of a magnitude capable of influencing institutional design.

Second, decision anchoring. If the costs of policy or institutional interventions (for example, mandatory pre-committed fallback plans, limits on the scope of irreversible delegation) can be estimated, the minimum effect size should be set to the level at which the intervention’s expected benefit exceeds its cost. At the laboratory stage, this anchoring can only take the form of a coarse sensitivity analysis: report the range of effect sizes within which power suffices to support the conclusion “the mechanism exists,” and the range that requires a larger sample or different design.

Third, methodological anchoring. Reference modern practice for smallest effect size of interest (SESOI) power analysis: the SESOI should be “the smallest effect that has practical or theoretical relevance in the specific field,” and SESOI-based power analysis should use simulation or interval-focused methods rather than relying solely on analytic formulas for null-hypothesis significance tests. For this study, that means the simulation in Appendix A should be reparameterized so that it reports power at the candidate SESOI, not power across an arbitrary set of \beta_3 values.

E.3 Current status

Version 0.2 has not completed this procedure. Section 7.7 lists it as a required step before preregistration. Until the three-source anchoring is complete, the estimate of N \approx 80 must be read strictly as: “under assumptions of \beta_3 = 0.5, 10 erroneous trials per cell, and a random-intercepts structure, the GLMM achieves approximately 87% power at N=80.” It is not a general sample-size recommendation.

