Appendix G: Design appendix for the competence-retention study

G.1 Why a separate design is required

Section 9 defines competence retention (CR) as the ratio of post-withdrawal performance to baseline performance and explicitly requires a separate power analysis for that study. The reason is structural: the primary experiment’s within-participant factors are latency and reversibility, whereas the competence-retention study introduces an additional within-participant phase (assisted → withdrawal), accompanied by order effects, practice effects, and potential selective attrition. Treating CR as a secondary outcome of the primary experiment would produce uninterpretable null results under conditions of insufficient power.

G.2 Recommended design elements

Phase structure. Baseline phase: no AI assistance, measuring independent performance H_{\text{baseline}}. Assisted phase: the AI-assisted task from the primary experiment or a simplified version, continued long enough to permit reliance patterns to form (exact length to be determined in piloting; reliance-pattern studies in the literature typically use multiple sessions rather than a single short task). Withdrawal phase: no AI assistance, measuring H_{\text{withdrawal}}. At least a short delay should be inserted between the assisted and withdrawal phases to reduce immediate working-memory carryover.

Order balancing. If participants complete multiple conditions (for example, high-delegation and low-delegation assisted phases), condition order must be balanced or randomized, and withdrawal-phase performance measured separately after each condition. If a between-participants design is used to avoid order effects, a larger sample is required, but causal interpretation is cleaner.

Measurement of reliance patterns. The d' and c indices from Appendix D remain applicable in the withdrawal phase, but another dimension is added: whether participants actively seek external verification. In the withdrawal phase, behavioral indicators of “whether the participant still attempts to call on external evidence or tools under no-AI conditions” are recommended. This connects to the augmentation versus substitution distinction in the AI epistemic disengagement literature: augmentation retains epistemic responsibility and conscientious evaluation, while substitution abdicates epistemic responsibility through uncritical acceptance. A decline in CR alone cannot distinguish these two patterns; behavioral indicators and calibration measures (whether participants’ confidence in their own answers matches actual accuracy) can.

Power-analysis requirements. The CR power analysis must use standardized difference or a time × condition interaction in a mixed model as the estimand, not the raw ratio. The raw ratio has an unstable sampling distribution when baseline performance approaches ceiling or floor; standardized differences (such as a within-participant Cohen’s d_z) or mixed-model interaction terms have more interpretable variance structures. The multiverse principle from Appendix F applies equally: the competence-retention study’s sample size should be chosen across plausible random-effects structures and attrition assumptions.

