Appendix H: Measurement framework for bridge conditions

H.1 Nature of bridge conditions

Section 10.1 lists five bridge conditions: cross-decision error correlation, institutional path dependence, erosion of the verification workforce, regulatory or competitive pressure, and institutional lock-in. These are conditions under which decision-level missed-correction rates may aggregate into institutional loss of corrective authority, not causal hypotheses that can be directly tested in the laboratory. Appendix H provides operationalization starting points for future research; it does not claim that these conditions have been measured in Version 0.2.

H.2 Draft operationalizations

Bridge condition Candidate measurement Data source
Cross-decision error correlation Whether errors by the same AI system across decisions share latent causes; intraclass correlation of error rates Audit logs; external-validity studies of error injection
Institutional path dependence Degree to which early decisions constrain the subsequent feasible option set; rate of option-set contraction over time Institutional case studies; archival analysis of decision sequences
Verification workforce erosion Change in the number, skill, and available time of independent verifiers as AI deployment intensity increases Organizational surveys; labor statistics; audit-capability assessments
Regulatory/competitive pressure Whether regulatory caps on review latency or market competition systematically compress human review time Regulatory filings; industry surveys; deployment timeline analysis
Institutional lock-in Estimated cost of replacing AI-generated decision pathways with alternatives; availability of alternatives Institutional cost assessments; technology-dependence audits

None of these measurements can be completed within a single laboratory experiment. They belong to institutional-level longitudinal research, and the contribution of Version 0.2 is to provide such research with hypotheses derived from decision-level mechanisms: if the bridge conditions hold, then the missed-correction mechanisms measured in Appendices B–F should leave detectable traces in institutional data.

