Appendix I: Mapping to adjacent frameworks

I.1 The seven evidence obligations of the Refusal Effectiveness Test

The Refusal Effectiveness Test (RET) decomposes effective human refusal into seven evidence obligations: authority, decision-relevant information, practical access, temporal sufficiency, causal efficacy, execution independence, and epistemic autonomy. This appendix provides a non-exhaustive mapping to clarify the overlap and divergence between Version 0.2 and RET.

RET evidence obligation Corresponding concept in this thesis Relation
Authority Distinction between formal authorization and effective corrective authority This thesis explicitly treats authority as necessary but insufficient
Decision-relevant information Distinction between independent verification and AI-assisted verification This thesis emphasizes that relevant information must be independent of the system being evaluated
Practical access Verification capacity and load This thesis reformulates this as a load-dependent capacity constraint
Temporal sufficiency \lambda and the intervention window This thesis treats this as a core experimental variable
Causal efficacy Intervention → containment/reversal → recovery This thesis decomposes this into four separately measurable stages
Execution independence Independent verification vs. correlated-system verification This thesis treats this as one component of verification capacity
Epistemic autonomy Competence retention; augmentation vs. substitution This thesis treats this as a separate research problem

RET’s central claim is stronger than this thesis’s: reversibility, not prior approval, should be the load-bearing safety guarantee. This thesis treats reversibility as one variable among several and tests its interaction with latency. The divergence is productive: if H1 is supported, RET’s strong claim gains decision-level mechanistic evidence; if H1 is rejected, RET’s reversibility-priority claim must at minimum be qualified to “conditions in which latency is short enough that the intervention window remains open.”

